    <article class="qt-prose qt-section">
        <h2>How the Indian number system works</h2>
        <p>Indian and international numbers use the same digits. What changes is where the commas go and what the groups are called. The international system puts a comma after every three digits: 1,500,000. The Indian system puts the first comma after three digits and then after every two: 15,00,000. Read the Indian version from the left and the words fall out on their own: 15 lakh.</p>
        <p>The reason is the units themselves. English counts in thousands, millions and billions, each a thousand times the last. Indian counting adds two extra stops, the lakh (one hundred thousand) and the crore (ten million), and those are only a hundred times apart. Grouping in twos after the thousands keeps the commas lined up with lakh and crore.</p>

        <h3>The same number, two ways</h3>
        <div class="qt-table-wrap mb-5">
            <table class="qt-table">
                <thead><tr><th>Indian grouping</th><th>Indian words</th><th>International grouping</th><th>International words</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td class="fig">1,000</td><td>One thousand</td><td class="fig">1,000</td><td>One thousand</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="fig">10,000</td><td>Ten thousand</td><td class="fig">10,000</td><td>Ten thousand</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="fig">1,00,000</td><td>One lakh</td><td class="fig">100,000</td><td>One hundred thousand</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="fig">10,00,000</td><td>Ten lakh</td><td class="fig">1,000,000</td><td>One million</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="fig">1,00,00,000</td><td>One crore</td><td class="fig">10,000,000</td><td>Ten million</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="fig">10,00,00,000</td><td>Ten crore</td><td class="fig">100,000,000</td><td>One hundred million</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="fig">1,00,00,00,000</td><td>One hundred crore (one arab)</td><td class="fig">1,000,000,000</td><td>One billion</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="fig">1,00,000 crore</td><td>One lakh crore</td><td class="fig">1,000,000,000,000</td><td>One trillion</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
        <p>Where you meet each system: Indian newspapers, the Union Budget and company annual reports use lakh and crore (a budget line of 1.5 lakh crore is 1.5 trillion). Startup funding news, global markets and most software use million and billion. When a US headline says 2 billion dollars, that is 200 crore dollars, and the rupee figure depends on the exchange rate of the day.</p>

        <h3>Conversion table with the number of zeros</h3>
        <div class="qt-table-wrap mb-5">
            <table class="qt-table">
                <thead><tr><th>Unit</th><th>Figure (Indian grouping)</th><th>Zeros</th><th>Equivalent</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><strong>Thousand</strong></td><td class="fig">1,000</td><td class="fig">3</td><td>One thousand</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Lakh</strong></td><td class="fig">1,00,000</td><td class="fig">5</td><td>100 thousand</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Million</strong></td><td class="fig">10,00,000</td><td class="fig">6</td><td>10 lakh</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Crore</strong></td><td class="fig">1,00,00,000</td><td class="fig">7</td><td>10 million</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Arab</strong></td><td class="fig">1,00,00,00,000</td><td class="fig">9</td><td>100 crore, or 1 billion</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Billion</strong></td><td class="fig">1,00,00,00,000</td><td class="fig">9</td><td>100 crore, or 1 arab</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Kharab</strong></td><td class="fig">1,00,00,00,00,000</td><td class="fig">11</td><td>10,000 crore, or 100 billion</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Trillion</strong></td><td class="fig">10,00,00,00,00,000</td><td class="fig">12</td><td>1 lakh crore</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
        <p>Arab and kharab are common in Hindi and Urdu speech but rare in English documents, which stop at crore and switch to lakh crore for larger amounts. Beyond kharab come neel (10<sup>13</sup>), padma (10<sup>15</sup>) and shankh (10<sup>17</sup>), which you will only meet in traditional texts.</p>
        <p>Reading budget or GDP figures such as 9.8 or 270 lakh crore? The <a href="/lakh-crore-in-numbers/">lakh crore in numbers table</a> has the digits, crore, trillion and zeros for the figures people look up most.</p>

        <h2>Writing an amount in words on a cheque</h2>
        <p>A cheque carries the amount twice: in figures in the box on the right and in words on the line below the payee name. The "On a cheque" line above gives you the words ready to copy. A few conventions make the cheque hard to alter and easy for the bank to read:</p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Start with Rupees and end with Only.</strong> Rupees One Lakh Fifty Thousand Only. The word Only stops anyone from adding to the amount, and it is the form banks expect.</li>
            <li><strong>Write paise after the rupees.</strong> Rupees One Thousand Five Hundred and Fifty Paise Only, with 1,500.50 in the figures box. If there are no paise, write Only straight after the rupee words.</li>
            <li><strong>Close the gaps.</strong> Draw a line through any blank space after Only, and write the figures close to the ₹ symbol with /- after them (1,50,000/-) so extra digits cannot be squeezed in.</li>
            <li><strong>Figures and words must agree.</strong> Section 18 of the Negotiable Instruments Act, 1881 says that when they differ, the amount in words is the amount payable. In practice a mismatched cheque is usually returned unpaid, so read both once more before signing.</li>
            <li><strong>No corrections.</strong> Under the RBI's CTS-2010 cheque standards, changes to the payee name or to the amount in figures or words are not accepted, even if you countersign them. Cancel the leaf and write a fresh one.</li>
            <li><strong>Date it right.</strong> A cheque is valid for three months from the date written on it (an RBI instruction in force since 1 April 2012). Write the date in the DD/MM/YYYY boxes printed on the leaf.</li>
        </ul>
        <h3>Examples</h3>
        <div class="qt-table-wrap mb-5">
            <table class="qt-table">
                <thead><tr><th>In figures</th><th>On the words line</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td class="fig">₹500/-</td><td>Rupees Five Hundred Only</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="fig">₹1,250.75</td><td>Rupees One Thousand Two Hundred Fifty and Seventy Five Paise Only</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="fig">₹1,50,000/-</td><td>Rupees One Lakh Fifty Thousand Only</td></tr>
