                    <tr><td class="fig">₹2,50,00,000/-</td><td>Rupees Two Crore Fifty Lakh Only</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
        <p>Our guide on <a href="/blog/how-to-write-cheques-india/">how to write a cheque in India</a> walks through the whole leaf, including account payee crossing and where to sign.</p>

        <h2>Amounts in words on invoices and GST bills</h2>
        <p>Most invoice formats print an Amount in Words line under the total, and accounting software fills it automatically. For Indian customers use the lakh and crore form that matches the figures on the bill: Rupees Twelve Lakh Thirty Four Thousand Only against ₹12,34,000. Export invoices are raised in the currency of the contract, so the words follow that currency and the international scale: US Dollars One Million Five Hundred Thousand Only. Keep one system per document. Mixing 15 lakh in one line and 1.5 million in the next invites errors.</p>
        <p>GST invoices are usually rounded to the nearest rupee (the CGST Act allows tax amounts to be rounded off to the nearest rupee), which means the words end with Only and there are no paise to spell out. Use the <a href="/gst-calculator/">GST calculator</a> to work out the CGST, SGST or IGST split before you write the total in words, and the <a href="/cash-counter/">cash counter</a> when the payment arrives in notes.</p>

        <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>1.5 lakh is not 15 lakh.</strong> 1.5 lakh is 1,50,000, while 15 lakh is 15,00,000. Decimal shorthand is fine in conversation, but write the full figure on documents.</li>
            <li><strong>Lakh, not lac.</strong> Both are understood, but lakh is the spelling in RBI and government usage and the one to put on official paperwork.</li>
            <li><strong>1.5 crore is 15 million, not 1.5 million.</strong> 1.5 million is only 15 lakh. Converting between the systems is where most reporting errors creep in, so check the zeros with the table above.</li>
            <li><strong>Hundred and one, or hundred one?</strong> Both forms appear on Indian cheques and both are read the same way. This converter leaves out the and inside the rupee amount and keeps it for the paise, which keeps the words unambiguous.</li>
            <li><strong>Lakh or lakhs?</strong> On the words line the singular is conventional (Fifteen Lakh Rupees), just as you would write Fifteen Thousand and not Fifteen Thousands.</li>
            <li><strong>Forgetting Only.</strong> Without it, a blank space at the end of the line is an invitation to add words.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Indian grouping in Excel and Google Sheets</h2>
        <p>Spreadsheets default to grouping in threes, and neither Excel nor Google Sheets has a built-in number to words function, which is why this page exists. For the commas, a custom number format switches a column to lakh and crore grouping:</p>
        <pre><code>[&gt;=10000000]##\,##\,##\,##0;[&gt;=100000]##\,##\,##0;##,##0</code></pre>
        <p>In Excel, select the cells, open Format Cells, pick Custom and paste the code. In Google Sheets, use Format, then Number, then Custom number format. The three sections handle crores, lakhs and smaller numbers in turn. Setting the Windows regional format to English (India) also makes Excel apply Indian grouping to its standard number formats.</p>

